## Problem

Shoppers describe what they want in sentences, for example "waterproof women's hiking boots under $150, size 8, 4 stars and up". Keyword search handles that badly. And AI search that guesses wrong is worse than having no AI search at all.

## What it does

- **Search in plain English.** Shoppers type a request like the one above. The search turns it into filters, such as price, size and rating.
- **Filters you can see and fix.** What the search understood appears as small filter tags. Shoppers can remove or edit a tag to correct it, without retyping.
- **Honest about what it missed.** Words that couldn't be matched to a filter are shown, so shoppers can see why the results look the way they do.
- **Everything a normal store search has.** Filter lists, sorting, pages of results and links you can share, like a real online store.

## How it helps your business

- **Shoppers can ask the way they talk,** instead of clicking through many filters one by one.
- **No hidden wrong guesses.** Shoppers always see what the search understood and can fix it.
- **Safe to improve.** Every change to the AI is tested for accuracy first, so search can't quietly get worse.
- **Not tied to one AI company.** It works with Claude or OpenAI, and a simple offline mode keeps the demo working without an AI account.

## How it's built

- **One filter schema.** A single Zod schema defines every filter the store supports. The model's output has to match it, or it's rejected.
- **Two providers and an offline mode.** Claude and OpenAI sit behind one small interface, and an offline parser keeps the demo working without API keys.
- **Evals.** An evaluation suite runs from the command line and scores the parser per provider.

## Stack

Next.js, TypeScript, Zod, the Claude and OpenAI APIs, and Tailwind CSS.

## Outcome

A public, runnable version of the natural-language filter pattern I've shipped in production, described in the [marketplace search project](/work/marketplace-search). You can try the demo, read the code, and run the evals yourself.
